{"availability":"available","full":"ecuber/tesseract","name":"tesseract","description":"Interactive visualization of a 4-dimensional hypercube.","category":"Other","language":"TypeScript","stars":7,"forks":2,"created":"2022-01-21T07:08:55Z","updated":"2022-01-24T02:25:32Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://tesseract22.netlify.app","topics":["4d","p5","projection","tesseract"],"demo":"https://tesseract22.netlify.app/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.624Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.624Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.624Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.624Z","stars":7,"forks":2}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["I created this visualization as an extra credit project for the multivariable calculus class I took in the fall of my senior year of high school.","A tesseract is essentially a 4D cube; what you see on the website is a visualization of the points of the tesseract undergoing two perspective projections): once from 4D into 3D, and again from 3D into 2D (which you see on your computer screen!)","The graphics were created with p5.js. I did not use the library's built-in WebGL 3D renderer. Rather, everything you see is represented by 2D shapes, the positions and scales of which I calculated manually (for whatever reason, doing something like this has been on my bucket list)."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/ecuber/tesseract/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"0ad9cfa5c8ae433ac294bf46792ebd89f948c83b6c5f2319564b9e837fb89b8e","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.792Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T01:10:34.792Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"I created this visualization as an extra credit project for the multivariable calculus class I took in the fall of my senior year of high school.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/ecuber/tesseract/blob/master/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint"